# Shell scripts must keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# A .sh file checked out with CRLF has a shebang reading "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r".
# The kernel then looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r", does not
# find one, and reports:
#
#     ./script.sh: command not found
#
# which points at the script rather than at its line endings and is why this
# wastes people's time. Authoring on Windows and running on macOS hits it every
# time without this file.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Same reasoning in reverse: Windows batch files want CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# PowerShell 5.1 reads .ps1 as ANSI unless there is a BOM, so keep these ASCII
# and CRLF to match the platform that runs them.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Leave everything else to git's own detection.
* text=auto
